From 521beee892d22a12c92078e2915c877ad83ef2d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 23:53:25 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 190/209] feat(area): Area function component over the legacy
 area proxy

"Area" is the second half of the split decision 01 asks for: the shape
components are pure geometry, and everything that used to be baked into a
legacy area object beside its shape - an id, a group, a priority, a near band,
an inner fade and a list of linked entities - lives here, on a function
component that binds by rule to whatever shape sits on the same entity.

How it drives the runtime (decision 03, research/07 option 1a)

It owns a hidden legacy IEntityAreaComponent, created with
GetOrCreateComponent and flagged NoSave + FLAG_NOT_SERIALIZE. That is the
whole reason nothing else in the engine had to change: the six area events,
the exclusive-group fade, the broad-phase grid, ExitAllAreas on teardown and
every consumer that looks an area up with GetProxy(ENTITY_PROXY_AREA) -
particles, the physics spline area, both audio components - keep working
against the same CArea they always used. NoSave keeps the level free of a
second copy of the geometry: CEntity::SerializeXML skips NoSave components, so
no <Area> node is written and the privileged <Area> hook in EntityLoadManager
has nothing to re-create on the next load.

It also attaches its own entity to that area (CArea::AddEntity(own id), the
BoidsProxy precedent). That is what makes the stock audio Environment and Area
components work when they are added to this same entity: neither looks for a
shape, both only require that their entity is in some area's attached list.
The self-move feedback loop that comes with self-attachment was fixed in the
engine one commit earlier.

An entity that already carries a legacy area proxy is left alone, with a
latched warning: taking it over would silently stop its <Area> node being
saved.

Geometry

The kinds describe themselves through two new, non-pure virtuals on
IShapeComponent - GetAreaVolume(SShapeAreaVolume&) and
GetContourObstruction() - so the Area component never downcasts and never
learns which kind it is talking to. A kind with no area volume (a spline is a
path, not a volume) returns false and is skipped with a warning. SShapeAreaVolume
is a flat POD and the contour travels in a caller-owned buffer: no container
crosses the interface (heap rule).

Pushing is split by how much changed. A moved point goes through the
MovePoints path added earlier, which updates the a2DSegments in place and
keeps the per-entity containment cache; a topology change, a box or a sphere
takes the full SetPoints / SetBox / SetSphere path. The ENTITY's own movement
is left to the legacy proxy's OnMove, which is already exactly those three
cheap per-kind pushes over the local geometry we last pushed - so there is no
second transform path that could disagree with it, and FLAG_NOT_UPDATE_AREA is
deliberately not set. A re-entrancy flag breaks the loop SetPoints would
otherwise close, because it broadcasts COMPONENT_PROPERTY_CHANGED to the whole
entity and the shape answers that with a change notification.

Links

Stored as a reflected wrapper around std::vector<CryGUID> (the SShapesArray
pattern), because a GUID is what survives a save and what the legacy <Area>
node stores. They resolve on ENTITY_EVENT_LEVEL_LOADED - the per-entity
equivalent of the instant CArea::ResolveEntityIds runs at - and, unlike
legacy, again on ENTITY_EVENT_LAYER_UNHIDE and on IEntitySystemSink::OnSpawn,
so a layer enabled after load and an entity spawned later still link. The
module keeps one spawn sink for every area rather than one per component, and
registers it with the entity system only while some area is waiting.

No per-frame cost: the component subscribes to no update event, and an idle
area is exactly the CArea the engine already had.

